[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"System Design Tutorial: Search Systems & Inverted Indexes (Elasticsearch, Lucene)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern search systems must process millions of documents and return relevant results in milliseconds. At the core of almost all high-performance text search engines like Elasticsearch, Apache Solr, and OpenSearch lies Apache Lucene and the concept of the inverted index."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Fundamental Concepts: Forward vs. Inverted Index"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a traditional relational database, data is stored as a forward index (Document-to-Terms mapping), which maps a document ID to its fields. While great for point lookups and aggregations, finding which documents contain a specific keyword requires scanning every single document, resulting in O(N) time complexity."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"An inverted index flips this relationship. It maps a Term (word) to a list of documents (and positions) where that term appears. This reduces search time complexity significantly."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Forward Index: Doc 1 -> "quick brown fox", Doc 2 -> "lazy dog""},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inverted Index: "quick" -> [Doc 1], "fox" -> [Doc 1], "dog" -> [Doc 2]"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Anatomy of Apache Lucene"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Apache Lucene is a high-performance, full-text search engine library written in Java. Elasticsearch and Solr are distributed wrappers built on top of Lucene. Lucene indexes are immutable and split into Segments."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Segments: A Lucene index consists of one or more immutable segments. When new documents are added, new segments are created. Periodically, these segments are merged in the background."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Term Dictionary & Postings List: The terms are stored sorted in a dictionary (often optimized using FST - Finite State Transducers), pointing to postings lists containing document IDs, term frequencies, and positions."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Elasticsearch Architecture & Scaling"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Elasticsearch scales Lucene by distributing data across multiple nodes using Indices, Shards, and Replicas. An index in Elasticsearch is a logical namespace pointing to one or more physical primary shards and zero or more replica shards."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"PUT /my_search_index
{
  "settings": {
    "number_of_shards": 3,
    "number_of_replicas": 1
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "title": { "type": "text" },
      "price": { "type": "float" }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. The Write and Read Path"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how data gets into the inverted index is crucial for system design capacity planning."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write Path: A write request hits a primary shard, is written to the translog (for durability), and buffered in memory. Periodically (every 1s), the buffer is refreshed to create a new Lucene segment in OS cache, making it searchable."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Path: A search request hits a coordinating node, which fans out the query to all shards (primary or replica). Each shard executes the query locally against its Lucene segments, returns local top-k results, and the coordinating node merges them into a global top-k response."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Best Practices for System Design Interviews"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Capacity Planning: Estimate document volume, average document size, and growth rate. Remember that an inverted index typically requires 20% to 50% of the raw data size, depending on text analysis and tokenization."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shard Sizing: Keep physical shard sizes between 10GB and 50GB to optimize garbage collection and segment merging."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Relevance Tuning: Use BM25 scoring algorithm parameters (k1, b) to tune search relevance based on business requirements."}]
